Z||000014||Monkeyman||01-07-2000||05:43 AM||||If they can make a set to fit the Fiero that's what I'll buy!  Those are cool!  Wow!  What a neat idea! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Not as luxurious as leather, but much more killer than cloth and cheaper than both.  And easier to keep clean than cloth, too.  Anyone know how well they wear?  ||||reg||1
Z||000015||Alex4mula||01-07-2000||11:03 AM||||Those Okele should look nice. For a more formal look you may try Mr. Mikes. I ordered mines gray with the Fiero logo embroyded (platinum). They look beautiful and fit perfectly. The kit comes with everytnig you need and a very nice video instructions. It took me about 2 hrs the 1st one (looking more at the VCR!) and 45 minutes the second. Quality and fit is excellent and looks better than factory. Nobodu beleives that I did it myself! I would recommend to anyone...||||reg||1
Z||000016||william_thorniii||01-07-2000||11:42 AM||||Alex,<P>Thanks for the information re: Mr. Mikes.  I enjoy the smell of leather, but do not like the looks or feel of it.  I drive a lot and cloth just breathes better in my opinion.  Mind you that leather can breathe but not well in this fashion. <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||4
Z||000017||Monkeyman||01-07-2000||08:35 PM||||Some of my seats would rather not breathe.  Especially in the Okole section, if you follow what I'm saying!||||reg||1
Z||000018||william_thorniii||01-07-2000||09:55 PM||||LOL<P>||||reg||1
Z||000019||Dave85||01-07-2000||11:41 PM||||William T,<BR>Before you order, if you do, keep in mind that neoprene DOES NOT breath at all.  The seat covers are very cool though. Gotta have them.<p>[This message has been edited by Dave85 (edited 01-07-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000020||Monkeyman||01-08-2000||06:48 AM||||Not meaning to sound like a smarta**, but does it matter if they breathe?  What good would it do if they did?  Seems to me that it would be better if they didn't.  Less dust and dirt to get inside the seat?  <P>William--You might ask about durability and wear so you don't get the same problem as you do in your '85 with the "stains"?||||reg||1
Z||000021||theogre||01-08-2000||03:05 PM||||Monkeyman, depends on how you look at it.<P>yeah, they would likely last longer in a none porouse material.<P>Problem comes when you have a long tirp to take. There's nothing to let air get around you and you start sweating and itching badly.||||reg||1
Z||000022||william_thorniii||01-08-2000||03:49 PM||||Monkey,<P>Ogre hit the nail on the head.  My drive to work is usually around 45 min.  I know that it isn't terribly long, but it is too long for a seat that does not breathe well.  Can you imagine wearing a shirt and tie and arriving all sweaty?  It is awful.  Also, since it is not a piled material (flat layer), the seat bottoms could not get thin from losing the fibers as the ones in my 85 GT have.<P>Thanks for the advice about neoprene.  I had already taken the fact that it doesn't breathe into consideration.  I ordered color swatches to try and determine if the seats can be milled to create paths for the air.  For durability concerns.  For those of you that don't know, neoprene is a rubber coated with a thin layer of nylon.  Nylon (in this form) is essentially impervious to stains, and rubber is virtually indestructable in this environment.  The only real problem that I could imagine would be UV damage.  Notice that this company sells some snake oil to prevent UV damage though.  I guess it would work, but you would do better with a sunshade thereby protecting your dash etc. too.<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||10
Z||000023||Monkeyman||01-08-2000||04:44 PM||||My bad.  I didn't think about the long trip thing.  The longest trip I make is about 3 hours to Ohio once a month or so.  I guess I should have thought about this.  (BTW, William--I can't even imagine wearing a tie, let alone what it would be like at the end of a 45 min ride!)  <P>William--How do you propose to "mill" the seat covers?  By this can I assume you would plan on doing some extra sewing to add some "valleys" in the covers?  It looks like they already did something like that.  Wonder if it is enough?  And what did you have in mind for a sunshade?  Just the generic type that folds/unfolds and sits in your windshield?  Or some kind of tint that's more permanent?  Does anyone make a very light tint with a reflectorized finish?  This might work for the windshield?  OK.  Enough questions for now.  Answer these and I'll ask some more.||||reg||1
Z||000024||Dave85||01-08-2000||11:03 PM||||With these seat covers I would expect to sweat if it was warm in the car. As a SCUBA diver I can tell you that neoprene gets hot quick! Within a minute of being in a wet suit you begin to sweat.  Don't get me wrong, I think the seat covers are cool and I plan to get a set. <BR>As far as the "snake oil",  it's the same stuff they sell in the SCUBA shops and I would recommend it.  UV rays would be the biggest problem(fading).<BR>Dave||||reg||1
Z||000025||william_thorniii||01-09-2000||02:21 AM||||Monkeyman,<P>I was actually thinking of taking it to a Bridgeport or more readily available drill press.  I don't know how familiar you are with neoprene, but it us usually about 1/4" (thick) of rubber... so I should have plenty of material to work with.  <P>On a sidenote, I just got finished with reading the February edition of C&D... Wet Okole has run advertisements in the back advertisement section of that magazine for years.  After I saw it today, I remembered seeing it over and over.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||10
Z||000026||Monkeyman||01-09-2000||05:49 AM||||William--Are you considering drilling holes in the fabric?  What would that do to the structural integrity of the material?  Anything?  If so, why would you need a drill press?  Couldn't you do the same thing with a hand drill?  I have access to a Bridgeport (which is acually a mill), but I don't see what the advantage would be to using that.  Explain this to me.||||reg||1
Z||000027||la9||01-09-2000||09:45 AM||||Just wondering if anyone knew if you could get some late model seats from a junk yard and retro-fit them ? From a Corvette or something and then the cushion would be in good shape also.||||reg||1
Z||000028||Monkeyman||01-09-2000||01:50 PM||||3rd gen F-body seats fit almost perfectly according to what I've read.  See Darklords site for more info.||||reg||1
Z||000029||william_thorniii||01-09-2000||06:01 PM||||Monkeyman,<P>since the material backing is rubber it shouldn't damage the structural integrity at all.  I had hoped to be able to leave the nylon in tact and only drill through the rubber.  This is why I wanted to use the Bridgeport.  A two-fluted end mill does not have a point and therefore I would not need to cut through the nylon fabric, only the rubber substrate.  I don't believe that it would be possible to completely perforate the entire cover without causing problems with the nylon unless you had some way to melt the nylon into a circular formation.  That is much too much work for $99 seat covers though.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000030||Monkeyman||01-09-2000||10:21 PM||||William-Check your figures as I'm sure I read that the custom made covers were more like $225.  I can see your point about using a mill, though.  It's a good idea although I think the same thing could be accomplished by using a hand drill.  Do they make milling bits for a hand drill?  If not, I'm sure I could "borrow" one from work and make it do.||||reg||1
Z||000031||william_thorniii||01-09-2000||10:58 PM||||Monkey,<P>You are correct about the $225 for custom covers.  However I had a hard time determining what the definition of custom is according to them.  I wrote them an e-mail the first day that I was told about the site, but have not received a reply as of yet.  Perhaps after you borrow that end-mill you could lend it to me since our shop closed down about a year ago... all of the equipt has subsequently been auctioned off.  Yes I think that a hand drill would work, but I would reccomend setting up some sort of fixture to ensure that you don't perforate the nylon.  <P>William T. Thorn, III<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||10
Z||000032||Monkeyman||01-10-2000||05:36 AM||||I'd love to let you "borrow" out Bridgeport, but since it's bolted to the floor and is brand new (and VERY expensive!) I'd probably get in trouble! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Ours is so cool.  Computer controlled (although they don't call it a CNC), 3 workstations, etc!!!  If I get these seat covers (probably will at some point) and the milling works, I might be able to mill your covers for a price (say...another 12-pack?!!! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">)||||reg||1
Z||000033||DJRice||01-10-2000||10:43 AM||||I have put f-body seats in two of my Fieros and they look and feel pretty good.  I got a tan set out of a 97 Firebird and a Grey set out of a 96 Camaro.  I replaced the tracks and rails that came with the seats with the original rails and had no problems.  The seats are a bit thicker.  Ive been meaning to snap a pic of the seats but keep forgetting.  Let me know if there is interest. (cuz Ill have to vacuum the car out first)||||reg||2
